WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.667
This video shows how a chat assistant gets the power to look things up and take actions.

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:11.092
A language model cannot read your database, call your fleet service, or reboot a device.

00:00:11.342 --> 00:00:21.663
Model: A trained file of numbers plus the math that uses them to turn an input into an output. For text, the input is your words and the output is a guess at the next word. Like: A compiled lookup engine: firmware image (the numbers) plus the code that reads it.

00:00:21.913 --> 00:00:23.759
It can only produce text.

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:29.733
Tool calling is the trick that lets that text become a safe, checked request your code chooses to run.

00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:43.880
Tool: A function your program lets the model request: search, read a file, query a database, run a test. The model never runs it itself. Like: A button on a panel the model may ask the operator to press.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:01:03.510
Tool calling (function calling): You describe functions to the model (name, purpose, parameters). Instead of answering in prose, the model can reply "call get_weather with city=Austin". Your code runs it and sends the result back. Like: The model writes a work order; your code is the technician who actually turns the screwdriver.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:08.918
Think of each tool as a remote procedure call that the model is allowed to request.

00:01:09.318 --> 00:01:16.678
You describe each tool: a name, one or two sentences on what it does and when to use it, and a schema for its arguments.

00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:32.404
JSON Schema: A standard JSON document that describes what valid JSON must look like: which fields, their types, which are required. Like: A C header file for data: it declares the struct so both sides agree on the layout.

00:01:32.704 --> 00:01:40.447
When the model wants data, it does not answer in prose. It replies with a structured request: this tool, these arguments.

00:01:40.847 --> 00:01:47.303
Your code decides whether to run it, runs it, and sends back the result. The model never executes anything itself.

00:01:48.503 --> 00:01:50.244
Here is a full round trip.

00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:55.381
Your code sends the question, together with descriptions of the available tools.

00:01:55.781 --> 00:02:02.225
The model answers with a tool call: get firmware, device D1. Each call carries an ID number.

00:02:02.625 --> 00:02:06.932
Your code validates the arguments, then calls your real fleet service.

00:02:07.332 --> 00:02:11.117
The service answers: installed version 2.1.3.

00:02:11.517 --> 00:02:18.634
Your code sends the history plus that result, tagged with the same call ID, so the model can match result to request.

00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:22.238
Now the model has facts, and writes the final answer.

00:02:23.438 --> 00:02:25.818
A few rules prevent most tool bugs.

00:02:26.218 --> 00:02:30.711
Validate the arguments before running anything. The model can invent values.

00:02:31.111 --> 00:02:37.369
Turn failures into error results the model can read, like unknown device ZZ, instead of crashing your loop.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:44.015
Every call gets exactly one result with its ID. Providers reject a conversation where they don't pair up.

00:02:44.415 --> 00:02:48.978
When the model asks for several independent calls at once, run them in parallel.

00:02:49.378 --> 00:02:55.020
And write tool descriptions like good API docs, because the model chooses tools by reading them.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:03.327
API: Application programming interface: the set of calls one program offers another. Here, usually an HTTP endpoint you send JSON to. Like: The ioctl list a driver exposes: the only doors into it.

00:03:04.377 --> 00:03:12.747
Question for you: The model requests set_led with color purple, but your schema only allows red, green or off. What should your code do?

00:03:19.047 --> 00:03:28.997
Answer: Do not run the tool and do not crash. Return an error result that says color must be one of red, green or off, so the model can correct its next call.

00:03:30.097 --> 00:03:30.747
Recap.

00:03:31.147 --> 00:03:34.572
A tool is a name, a description and an argument schema.

00:03:34.972 --> 00:03:38.223
The model requests, and your code validates and runs.

00:03:38.623 --> 00:03:41.212
Results go back with the matching call ID.

00:03:41.612 --> 00:03:47.823
And errors are data for the model, not crashes. Lab four builds a tool registry that does all of this.
